<Base
  title={'Risk analytics case study — Felipe Alves'}
  description={"End-to-end delivery of an integrated risk-analysis framework in Brazil's regulated electricity market."}
  ogDescription={'Requirements, SQL, ETL, analytics and decision support for an integrated risk-analysis framework.'}
  ogType="article"
  css={['styles', 'responsive', 'enhancements', 'site-fixes', 'illustrations']}
  bodyClass="case-page"
  script="script"
>
<main><section class="case-hero section-shell reveal"><div><span class="eyebrow">Case study 03 · Risk analytics</span><h1>Turning fragmented compliance signals into integrated risk analysis.</h1><p>End-to-end delivery of an analytical framework connecting Internal Audit, Market Security and technical teams inside Brazil's regulated electricity market.</p></div><div class="case-facts"><div><span>Role</span><strong>Business Analyst</strong></div><div><span>Domain</span><strong>Risk · compliance · energy market</strong></div><div><span>Outcome</span><strong>32% reduction in compliance risk</strong></div><div><span>Stack</span><strong>SQL · ETL · SAS · BI PoCs</strong></div></div></section><section class="case-body section-shell"><aside class="case-nav"><a href="#context">Context</a><a href="#problem">Problem</a><a href="#approach">Approach</a><a href="#outcome">Outcome</a><a href="#illustration">Illustration</a><a href="#ownership">Ownership</a><a href="#public-context">Public context</a><a href="#reflection">Reflection</a><a class="back-link" href="/#work">← Back to work</a></aside><div><section class="case-section" id="context"><span class="eyebrow">Context</span><h2>Regulation creates data problems before it creates dashboard problems.</h2><p>At CCEE, market and compliance decisions sit on top of complex rules, systems and data. Internal Audit, Market Security and technology teams each held different parts of the picture. The analytical challenge was to connect them into a repeatable risk-analysis framework.</p></section><section class="case-section" id="problem"><span class="eyebrow">Problem</span><h2>Fragmented signals make risk harder to see and harder to act on.</h2><p>The work required translating risk and compliance requirements into something technically implementable: data definitions, transformations, analytical rules, reporting needs and a structure that could support investigation rather than one-off manual analysis.</p></section><section class="case-section" id="approach"><span class="eyebrow">Approach</span><h2>Bridge the risk question all the way to the data layer.</h2><div class="decision-grid"><div class="decision-card"><span>Requirements</span><h3>Translate risk intent</h3><p>Worked across Internal Audit, Market Security and technical stakeholders to turn compliance concerns into explicit analytical requirements.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><span>Data</span><h3>Shape the analytical foundation</h3><p>Contributed to data modelling and ETL design, using advanced SQL for extraction, transformation, investigation and solution design.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><span>Analytics</span><h3>Support implementation</h3><p>Contributed to SAS analytics implementation and ran proof-of-concepts using Python, Power BI and Tableau.</p></div><div class="decision-card"><span>Decision support</span><h3>Make findings usable</h3><p>Developed executive-level data storytelling and recommendations so analytical findings could influence action.</p></div></div></section><section class="case-section" id="outcome"><span class="eyebrow">Outcome</span><h2>A measurable reduction in compliance risk.</h2><div class="evidence-strip"><div class="evidence-pill">−32% compliance-risk measure</div><div class="evidence-pill">End-to-end framework delivery</div><div class="evidence-pill">Advanced SQL</div><div class="evidence-pill">Data modelling + ETL</div><div class="evidence-pill">Executive storytelling</div></div><p>The 32% figure is the verified internal result associated with the Integrated Risk Analysis framework. The implementation was cross-functional; my role was to lead end-to-end delivery and bridge the business, analytical and technical pieces.</p></section><RiskSignalsIllustration /><section class="case-section" id="ownership"><span class="eyebrow">Ownership</span><h2>Business analysis with technical depth.</h2><div class="ownership-box"><div><h3>I led</h3><ul><li>End-to-end framework delivery</li><li>Requirements and stakeholder translation</li><li>Risk / compliance reporting definitions</li><li>Analytical PoCs and executive storytelling</li></ul></div><div><h3>I contributed with specialists</h3><ul><li>Data modelling</li><li>ETL design</li><li>SAS analytics implementation</li><li>Technical productionisation</li></ul></div></div></section><section class="case-section public-context" id="public-context"><span class="eyebrow">Independent public context</span><h2>The framework continued into a regulated market-security capability.</h2><p class="context-intro">I left CCEE in September 2022. Public sources show the framework's later evolution and operating context.</p><div class="source-grid"><a class="source-card" href="https://www.ccee.org.br/documents/80415/919440/20230316_Apresenta%C3%A7%C3%A3o_EncontroCCEE_Madalena.pdf/635a16e3-7eb6-cb47-7f46-f53fc122ef56" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span class="source-type">CCEE · 2023</span><strong>60+ metrics · 10 indicators · 3 pillars</strong><span>Public ARI operating model: commercial, financial and operational ↗</span></a><a class="source-card" href="https://www.ccee.org.br/en/web/guest/monitoramento-prudencial" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span class="source-type">CCEE · current framework</span><strong>Prudential monitoring became formal market infrastructure</strong><span>REN 1.072/2023 and leverage-factor declarations ↗</span></a><a class="source-card" href="https://www.ccee.org.br/documents/80415/26443125/Manual%20Monitoramento%20Prudencial%20v2.pdf/5a801bf2-923b-1ecc-60aa-fd3446f571d1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span class="source-type">CCEE · public methodology</span><strong>The methodology is documented publicly</strong><span>Historical proposal, risk concepts and calculation premises ↗</span></a></div></section><section class="case-section" id="reflection"><span class="eyebrow">Reflection</span><h2>The strongest analysts often work before the dashboard.</h2><p>The reusable lesson was that decision quality depends on definitions and data structure long before visualisation. My SQL and systems background made it possible to challenge the problem at that level instead of treating analytics as a presentation layer.</p></section><section class="case-cta"><span class="eyebrow">Next</span><h2>Want to talk this through?</h2><div class="closing-actions"><a class="button primary" href="mailto:felipe.s.alves7@gmail.com">Email Felipe</a><a class="button ghost" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/felipealves7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn ↗</a><a class="button ghost" href="/#work">More selected work</a></div></section></div></section></main>
</Base>
